tories we collect and make with plants are the ones that build a relationship with the living world. So, I wish to conclude this interview with one last question; with all your experiences and projects with plants what do you think is the most important piece of transformative advice you could give to humans about building deeper connections with plants? Yuxi: As for advice on building connections with plants, I would say always be respectful, mindful, and stay curious. Take the time to observe plants closely, learn about their needs and behaviors, and appreciate their vital role in our ecosystem. By nurturing plants, we not only foster their growth but also cultivate a deeper sense of connection to the natural world. This connection can transform our perception of nature from something external to something we are intrinsically a part of. Engage with plants not just as objects of beauty or utility but as living beings with their own intrinsic value. This shift in perspective can lead to more sustainable and harmonious ways of living, benefiting both ourselves and the planet. Last but not least, the above bits of advice goes beyond just plants to the more-than-human world in general. It is crucial to find one’s own way of being with the more-than-human, observing, learning, and understanding. It’s always about perspective shifting. Anna Maria: Thank you for all your time and consideration. It was a pleasure to learn more about your work Yuxi: Thank you! It’s been a pleasure discussing my work, and I look forward to continuing the conversation in the future.', 'Sources/Raw/designposthumanism/post-2400-micro-interview-no-12-anna-maria-orru-meets-yuxi-chen-e8b08c-e7a6b9e8a5bf.md', 'designposthumanism', 'https://designandposthumanism.org/archives/2400');
INSERT INTO source_chunks(id, title, url, source_family, path, body, description) VALUES (52, 'D+PH Network Session #1', 'https://designandposthumanism.org/archives/2407', 'designposthumanism', 'Sources/Raw/designposthumanism/post-2407-dph-network-session-1.md', '# D+PH Network Session #1 We are excited to announce that the Design and Posthumanism Network is organizing our first public online event on October 10th, and we are now seeking speakers/presenters to share insights on how design and posthumanism intersect in practice. This event will explore the practical applications and implications of critical posthumanist principles in design, providing a platform for thought leaders and practitioners to exchange ideas. We are looking to invite the network and people who are curious to the event. We are looking for three engaging presentations. If you have an interesting project or insights in this area and are interested in presenting, we invite you to apply for a speaking opportunity. This is a fantastic chance to showcase your work and contribute to a meaningful discussion. To apply, please sign up and submit your proposal through the following form by clicking here. The deadline for submissions is Sunday 25.8.2024. We hope you already mark your calendars for 10th October between 17-20 CET. We look forward to the possibility of including your unique perspective in our program. We will go through the suggestions together with the event team and see which presentations would make a great combo for our first public event. We are meeting 30th August to plan the agenda. If you have any questions or require further information, please do not hesitate to contact the event planning team. GO TO THE *network sessions* PAGE FOR FURTHER INFORMATION The event planning team: Satu Samira Hamed / satu@planetdiplomats.com Anton Poikolainen Rosén / anton.poikolainenrosen@aalto.fi Sebastian Gatz / Sebastian.Gatz@konstfack.se', '# D+PH Network Session #1 We are excited to announce that the Design and Posthumanism Network is organizing our first public online event on October 10th, and we are now seeking speakers/presenters to share insights on how design and posthumanism intersect in practice. This event will explore the practical applications and implications of critical posthumanist principles in design, providing a platform for thought leaders and practitioners to exchange ideas. We are looking to invite the network and people who are curious to the event. We are looking for three engaging presentations. If you have an interesting project or insights in this area and are interested in presenting, we invite you to apply for a speaking opportunity. This is a fantastic chance to showcase your work and contribute to a meaningful discussion. To apply, please sign up and submit your proposal through the following f');
INSERT INTO source_chunks_fts(title, body, path, source_family, url) VALUES ('D+PH Network Session #1', '# D+PH Network Session #1 We are excited to announce that the Design and Posthumanism Network is organizing our first public online event on October 10th, and we are now seeking speakers/presenters to share insights on how design and posthumanism intersect in practice. This event will explore the practical applications and implications of critical posthumanist principles in design, providing a platform for thought leaders and practitioners to exchange ideas. We are looking to invite the network and people who are curious to the event. We are looking for three engaging presentations. If you have an interesting project or insights in this area and are interested in presenting, we invite you to apply for a speaking opportunity. This is a fantastic chance to showcase your work and contribute to a meaningful discussion. To apply, please sign up and submit your proposal through the following form by clicking here. The deadline for submissions is Sunday 25.8.2024. We hope you already mark your calendars for 10th October between 17-20 CET. We look forward to the possibility of including your unique perspective in our program. We will go through the suggestions together with the event team and see which presentations would make a great combo for our first public event. We are meeting 30th August to plan the agenda. If you have any questions or require further information, please do not hesitate to contact the event planning team. GO TO THE *network sessions* PAGE FOR FURTHER INFORMATION The event planning team: Satu Samira Hamed / satu@planetdiplomats.com Anton Poikolainen Rosén / anton.poikolainenrosen@aalto.fi Sebastian Gatz / Sebastian.Gatz@konstfack.se', 'Sources/Raw/designposthumanism/post-2407-dph-network-session-1.md', 'designposthumanism', 'https://designandposthumanism.org/archives/2407');
INSERT INTO source_chunks(id, title, url, source_family, path, body, description) VALUES (53, 'Rat-ical Justice – Care at the Interface of the Human and the Nonhuman', 'https://designandposthumanism.org/archives/2420', 'designposthumanism', 'Sources/Raw/designposthumanism/post-2420-rat-ical-justice-care-at-the-interface-of-the-human-and-the-nonhuman.md', '# Rat-ical Justice – Care at the Interface of the Human and the Nonhuman Coincident with the development of human civilization was the emergence of a new type of critter: one not domesticated, not beloved, but similar to us in so many aspects that they abound in our environments—the urban rat. This urban critter burgeons in numbers that we humans find threatening if not overtly problematic. Ironically, this “problem” is one of our own creation; our actions are what have allowed these critters to thrive. But instead of assuming responsibility for inviting them to the table, we blame them for arriving at our doorstep and then poison their dinner. Using the widespread mistreatment of rats as an example, this thesis critiques the anthropocentric worldview that enables the abuse of marginalized individuals and absolves us of accountability for our actions. It argues that a fundamental shift away from this worldview is necessary in relearning how to exist in a world of others. It also aims to propose ways to initiate this shift toward respectful cohabitation through the exploration of various social and spatial facilitators. By utilizing four tactics—strategic anthropomorphism, public engagement, the mapping of heterotopias, and negotiation—this work explores how practices of care can showcase the perspectives of neglected nonhumans and facilitate respectful cohabitation between rats and humans in an urban environment. The outcome of this thesis is a series of experiments that address various aspects of our relationship with urban rats. Key findings include a collection of strategies for learning how to foster empathy for rats, efforts aimed at advocating for their rights as urban inhabitants, and the showcasing of the homes they make in our cities’ in-between spaces. Furthermore, this thesis results in experimental negotiations that imagine spaces that compromise rather than utilize traditional means of control to enable respectful cohabitation in the urban landscape. Rat-ical Justice shows that empathy and care can, and indeed should, be extended to every individual, human or nonhuman. A Master’s Thesis by Delaney Davis & Lisa Säfdal, at the Master’s Program: “Architecture and Planning Beyond Sustainability” at Chalmers School of Architecture, Gothenburg, Sweden. Thesis', '# Rat-ical Justice – Care at the Interface of the Human and the Nonhuman Coincident with the development of human civilization was the emergence of a new type of critter: one not domesticated, not beloved, but similar to us in so many aspects that they abound in our environments—the urban rat. This urban critter burgeons in numbers that we humans find threatening if not overtly problematic. Ironically, this “problem” is one of our own creation; our actions are what have allowed these critters to thrive. But instead of assuming responsibility for inviting them to the table, we blame them for arriving at our doorstep and then poison their dinner. Using the widespread mistreatment of rats as an example, this thesis critiques the anthropocentric worldview that enables the abuse of marginalized individuals and absolves us of accountability for our actions. It argues that a fundamental shift a');
INSERT INTO source_chunks_fts(title, body, path, source_family, url) VALUES ('Rat-ical Justice – Care at the Interface of the Human and the Nonhuman', '# Rat-ical Justice – Care at the Interface of the Human and the Nonhuman Coincident with the development of human civilization was the emergence of a new type of critter: one not domesticated, not beloved, but similar to us in so many aspects that they abound in our environments—the urban rat. This urban critter burgeons in numbers that we humans find threatening if not overtly problematic. Ironically, this “problem” is one of our own creation; our actions are what have allowed these critters to thrive. But instead of assuming responsibility for inviting them to the table, we blame them for arriving at our doorstep and then poison their dinner. Using the widespread mistreatment of rats as an example, this thesis critiques the anthropocentric worldview that enables the abuse of marginalized individuals and absolves us of accountability for our actions. It argues that a fundamental shift away from this worldview is necessary in relearning how to exist in a world of others. It also aims to propose ways to initiate this shift toward respectful cohabitation through the exploration of various social and spatial facilitators. By utilizing four tactics—strategic anthropomorphism, public engagement, the mapping of heterotopias, and negotiation—this work explores how practices of care can showcase the perspectives of neglected nonhumans and facilitate respectful cohabitation between rats and humans in an urban environment. The outcome of this thesis is a series of experiments that address various aspects of our relationship with urban rats. Key findings include a collection of strategies for learning how to foster empathy for rats, efforts aimed at advocating for their rights as urban inhabitants, and the showcasing of the homes they make in our cities’ in-between spaces. Furthermore, this thesis results in experimental negotiations that imagine spaces that compromise rather than utilize traditional means of control to enable respectful cohabitation in the urban landscape. Rat-ical Justice shows that empathy and care can, and indeed should, be extended to every individual, human or nonhuman. A Master’s Thesis by Delaney Davis & Lisa Säfdal, at the Master’s Program: “Architecture and Planning Beyond Sustainability” at Chalmers School of Architecture, Gothenburg, Sweden. Thesis', 'Sources/Raw/designposthumanism/post-2420-rat-ical-justice-care-at-the-interface-of-the-human-and-the-nonhuman.md', 'designposthumanism', 'https://designandposthumanism.org/archives/2420');
INSERT INTO source_chunks(id, title, url, source_family, path, body, description) VALUES (54, 'Confluence of European Water Bodies', 'https://designandposthumanism.org/archives/2690', 'designposthumanism', 'Sources/Raw/designposthumanism/post-2690-confluence-of-european-water-bodies.md', '# Confluence of European Water Bodies The Confluence of European Water Bodies is coming together this October at Ocean Space in Venice, bringing together more than 25 grassroots communities of artists, activists, lawyers and ecologists, representing seas, lakes, rivers, lagoons and glaciers from all over Europe. From October 3 to 6, 2024, Ocean Space will host panel discussions, meetings with local activists, performances, screenings, and more, dedicated to a collective pursuit of a deeper understanding of the ‘Rights of Nature’ in Europe from a cultural perspective. Make sure not to miss the public program at Ocean Space: Thursday, October 3, 2024, 19.30 pm: Screening of I Am the River, the River is Me Sunday, October 6, 2024, 10 am: Can the Piave Still Murmur? Assembly for the Grave di Ciano The Confluence of European Water Bodies is founded by the Embassy of the North Sea, ILP Mar Menor and TBA21–Academy and supported by the Ca’ Foscari University of Venice: NICHE (THE NEW INSTITUTE Center for Environmental Humanities), GARN (Global Alliance for the Rights of Nature), the Italian Buddhist Union, Creative Industries Fund NL, and Venice Climate Change Pavilion. #confluenceofeuropeanwaterbodies #rightsofnature #waterbodies See more about the project at water-bodies.eu. If you are interested in the Confluence of European Water Bodies, reach out to network members Thomas Laurien or Christoph Matt who will represent waterbodies at the confluence in Venice.', '# Confluence of European Water Bodies The Confluence of European Water Bodies is coming together this October at Ocean Space in Venice, bringing together more than 25 grassroots communities of artists, activists, lawyers and ecologists, representing seas, lakes, rivers, lagoons and glaciers from all over Europe. From October 3 to 6, 2024, Ocean Space will host panel discussions, meetings with local activists, performances, screenings, and more, dedicated to a collective pursuit of a deeper understanding of the ‘Rights of Nature’ in Europe from a cultural perspective. Make sure not to miss the public program at Ocean Space: Thursday, October 3, 2024, 19.30 pm: Screening of I Am the River, the River is Me Sunday, October 6, 2024, 10 am: Can the Piave Still Murmur? Assembly for the Grave di Ciano The Confluence of European Water Bodies is founded by the Embassy of the North Sea, ILP Mar Men');
INSERT INTO source_chunks_fts(title, body, path, source_family, url) VALUES ('Confluence of European Water Bodies', '# Confluence of European Water Bodies The Confluence of European Water Bodies is coming together this October at Ocean Space in Venice, bringing together more than 25 grassroots communities of artists, activists, lawyers and ecologists, representing seas, lakes, rivers, lagoons and glaciers from all over Europe. From October 3 to 6, 2024, Ocean Space will host panel discussions, meetings with local activists, performances, screenings, and more, dedicated to a collective pursuit of a deeper understanding of the ‘Rights of Nature’ in Europe from a cultural perspective. Make sure not to miss the public program at Ocean Space: Thursday, October 3, 2024, 19.30 pm: Screening of I Am the River, the River is Me Sunday, October 6, 2024, 10 am: Can the Piave Still Murmur? Assembly for the Grave di Ciano The Confluence of European Water Bodies is founded by the Embassy of the North Sea, ILP Mar Menor and TBA21–Academy and supported by the Ca’ Foscari University of Venice: NICHE (THE NEW INSTITUTE Center for Environmental Humanities), GARN (Global Alliance for the Rights of Nature), the Italian Buddhist Union, Creative Industries Fund NL, and Venice Climate Change Pavilion. #confluenceofeuropeanwaterbodies #rightsofnature #waterbodies See more about the project at water-bodies.eu. If you are interested in the Confluence of European Water Bodies, reach out to network members Thomas Laurien or Christoph Matt who will represent waterbodies at the confluence in Venice.', 'Sources/Raw/designposthumanism/post-2690-confluence-of-european-water-bodies.md', 'designposthumanism', 'https://designandposthumanism.org/archives/2690');
INSERT INTO source_chunks(id, title, url, source_family, path, body, description) VALUES (55, 'Network Sessions #1: WATER', 'https://designandposthumanism.org/archives/2724', 'designposthumanism', 'Sources/Raw/designposthumanism/post-2724-network-sessions-1-water.md', '# Network Sessions #1: WATER Thursday, October 10, 2024 5-8pm (CEST) The theme of the first public event of the Design + Posthumanism Network is water, since water captures both the concreteness and philosophical underpinnings of posthuman design. Concretely, all life on earth needs water for its survival – and the uneven distribution of freshwater makes evident global inequalities. Metaphorically, water lends itself for discussing posthuman concepts of connection, interdependence, transformation, movement and leakage. We invite you to three hours of flowing, melting, and leaking presentations and discussions. sign up for the event here AGENDA 5:00pm Introduction to posthumanist design and the Design and Posthumanism Network 5.30pm Act 1 – FLOWING Negotiating Entangled Encounters on a More-Than-Human Planet 5:55pm Act 2 – MELTING ICE * In Case of Emergency by Sympoietic Society 6.20pm Act 3 – LEAKING Frictional water practices in the Global South 6.45pm Workshop and networking 8.00pm Event ends LINK TO THE PRESENTATIONS The event planning team: Satu Samira Hamed / satu@planetdiplomats.com Anton Poikolainen Rosén / anton.poikolainenrosen@aalto.fi Sebastian Gatz / Sebastian.Gatz@konstfack.se', '# Network Sessions #1: WATER Thursday, October 10, 2024 5-8pm (CEST) The theme of the first public event of the Design + Posthumanism Network is water, since water captures both the concreteness and philosophical underpinnings of posthuman design. Concretely, all life on earth needs water for its survival – and the uneven distribution of freshwater makes evident global inequalities. Metaphorically, water lends itself for discussing posthuman concepts of connection, interdependence, transformation, movement and leakage. We invite you to three hours of flowing, melting, and leaking presentations and discussions. sign up for the event here AGENDA 5:00pm Introduction to posthumanist design and the Design and Posthumanism Network 5.30pm Act 1 – FLOWING Negotiating Entangled Encounters on a More-Than-Human Planet 5:55pm Act 2 – MELTING ICE * In Case of Emergency by Sympoietic Society 6.20pm Ac');
INSERT INTO source_chunks_fts(title, body, path, source_family, url) VALUES ('Network Sessions #1: WATER', '# Network Sessions #1: WATER Thursday, October 10, 2024 5-8pm (CEST) The theme of the first public event of the Design + Posthumanism Network is water, since water captures both the concreteness and philosophical underpinnings of posthuman design. Concretely, all life on earth needs water for its survival – and the uneven distribution of freshwater makes evident global inequalities. Metaphorically, water lends itself for discussing posthuman concepts of connection, interdependence, transformation, movement and leakage. We invite you to three hours of flowing, melting, and leaking presentations and discussions. sign up for the event here AGENDA 5:00pm Introduction to posthumanist design and the Design and Posthumanism Network 5.30pm Act 1 – FLOWING Negotiating Entangled Encounters on a More-Than-Human Planet 5:55pm Act 2 – MELTING ICE * In Case of Emergency by Sympoietic Society 6.20pm Act 3 – LEAKING Frictional water practices in the Global South 6.45pm Workshop and networking 8.00pm Event ends LINK TO THE PRESENTATIONS The event planning team: Satu Samira Hamed / satu@planetdiplomats.com Anton Poikolainen Rosén / anton.poikolainenrosen@aalto.fi Sebastian Gatz / Sebastian.Gatz@konstfack.se', 'Sources/Raw/designposthumanism/post-2724-network-sessions-1-water.md', 'designposthumanism', 'https://designandposthumanism.org/archives/2724');
INSERT INTO source_chunks(id, title, url, source_family, path, body, description) VALUES (56, 'STS Hub 2025 / Diffracting the Critical / Panel: Fieldscapes – A situated approach to multispecies design', 'https://designandposthumanism.org/archives/2734', 'designposthumanism', 'Sources/Raw/designposthumanism/post-2734-sts-hub-2025-diffracting-the-critical-panel-fieldscapes-a-situated-approach-to-m.md', '# STS Hub 2025 / Diffracting the Critical / Panel: Fieldscapes – A situated approach to multispecies design Interspecies interactions are intensely explored in Multispecies Studies, with special attention to the challenges posed by contemporary poly-crises. Scholars like Ávila (2022) emphasize the need for conscious approaches in design research to acknowledge multispecies interdependence and promote practices of care and coexistence for a more equitable (Holland & Roudavski 2024), cosmopolitical (Yaneva & Zaera-Polo 2015), and regenerative “Pluriverse” (Escobar 2018). Bringing these theoretical insights into practice poses critical challenges. Biodesign (Myers & Antonelli 2012), an interdisciplinary field engaging with living organisms—from bacteria to corals—explores new ‘living’ materialities (Karana, et al. 2020) through prototypes, artifacts, and architectural structures. While intense collaboration takes place with biotech disciplines, there is limited inclusion of other-than-academic, vernacular, and tacit knowledge, such as cultural craft techniques and field-based multispecies ontologies. We advocate for fieldwork as an underexplored diffracting method that can enhance multispecies sensibilities, deepen engagements with situated configurations and ecologies, and foster reflexivity and sensitivity in Biodesign processes. This panel seeks to elaborate on experimental field-based contributions to Biodesign. We aim to explore how ethnographic methods, practice-based designerly ways of knowing, and development of knowledge in actu (Mareis 2017) can contribute to the study of interspecies interactions in the field. We also want to examine how prototyping can become a diffractive practice, engaging with the flux of materialities (Ingold 2007) and organisms in situated contexts. Furthermore, we are interested in how Biodesign-artifacts can foster invitational practices (Lindström and Ståhl 2020) through regenerative socio-materialities in situated configurations and ecologies. We welcome critical discussions on epistemological, ontological, and praxiological translations across diverse sites (Latour 1983), attempting to bridge academic and other-than-academic and disciplinary boundaries. Emma Sicher and Rasa Weber (emma.sicher@gmail.com) Conference Call and template First deadline: October 31st Abstract 200 words + Author(s) bio(s) 75 words Second deadline (after selection): January 10th Short paper 1000-2000 words', '# STS Hub 2025 / Diffracting the Critical / Panel: Fieldscapes – A situated approach to multispecies design Interspecies interactions are intensely explored in Multispecies Studies, with special attention to the challenges posed by contemporary poly-crises. Scholars like Ávila (2022) emphasize the need for conscious approaches in design research to acknowledge multispecies interdependence and promote practices of care and coexistence for a more equitable (Holland & Roudavski 2024), cosmopolitical (Yaneva & Zaera-Polo 2015), and regenerative “Pluriverse” (Escobar 2018). Bringing these theoretical insights into practice poses critical challenges. Biodesign (Myers & Antonelli 2012), an interdisciplinary field engaging with living organisms—from bacteria to corals—explores new ‘living’ materialities (Karana, et al. 2020) through prototypes, artifacts, and architectural structures. While inte');
INSERT INTO source_chunks_fts(title, body, path, source_family, url) VALUES ('STS Hub 2025 / Diffracting the Critical / Panel: Fieldscapes – A situated approach to multispecies design', '# STS Hub 2025 / Diffracting the Critical / Panel: Fieldscapes – A situated approach to multispecies design Interspecies interactions are intensely explored in Multispecies Studies, with special attention to the challenges posed by contemporary poly-crises. Scholars like Ávila (2022) emphasize the need for conscious approaches in design research to acknowledge multispecies interdependence and promote practices of care and coexistence for a more equitable (Holland & Roudavski 2024), cosmopolitical (Yaneva & Zaera-Polo 2015), and regenerative “Pluriverse” (Escobar 2018). Bringing these theoretical insights into practice poses critical challenges. Biodesign (Myers & Antonelli 2012), an interdisciplinary field engaging with living organisms—from bacteria to corals—explores new ‘living’ materialities (Karana, et al. 2020) through prototypes, artifacts, and architectural structures. While intense collaboration takes place with biotech disciplines, there is limited inclusion of other-than-academic, vernacular, and tacit knowledge, such as cultural craft techniques and field-based multispecies ontologies. We advocate for fieldwork as an underexplored diffracting method that can enhance multispecies sensibilities, deepen engagements with situated configurations and ecologies, and foster reflexivity and sensitivity in Biodesign processes. This panel seeks to elaborate on experimental field-based contributions to Biodesign. We aim to explore how ethnographic methods, practice-based designerly ways of knowing, and development of knowledge in actu (Mareis 2017) can contribute to the study of interspecies interactions in the field. We also want to examine how prototyping can become a diffractive practice, engaging with the flux of materialities (Ingold 2007) and organisms in situated contexts. Furthermore, we are interested in how Biodesign-artifacts can foster invitational practices (Lindström and Ståhl 2020) through regenerative socio-materialities in situated configurations and ecologies. We welcome critical discussions on epistemological, ontological, and praxiological translations across diverse sites (Latour 1983), attempting to bridge academic and other-than-academic and disciplinary boundaries. Emma Sicher and Rasa Weber (emma.sicher@gmail.com) Conference Call and template First deadline: October 31st Abstract 200 words + Author(s) bio(s) 75 words Second deadline (after selection): January 10th Short paper 1000-2000 words', 'Sources/Raw/designposthumanism/post-2734-sts-hub-2025-diffracting-the-critical-panel-fieldscapes-a-situated-approach-to-m.md', 'designposthumanism', 'https://designandposthumanism.org/archives/2734');
INSERT INTO source_chunks(id, title, url, source_family, path, body, description) VALUES (57, 'Mineral Matterings: Diffractive Practices of Design', 'https://designandposthumanism.org/archives/2762', 'designposthumanism', 'Sources/Raw/designposthumanism/post-2762-mineral-matterings-diffractive-practices-of-design.md', '# Mineral Matterings: Diffractive Practices of Design PETRA LILJA: PUBLIC DEFENSE OF DOCTORAL DISSERTATION This dissertation, Mineral Matterings: Diffractive Practices of Design makes design perceptible as a source of concern, part of the extractivist logic. Through two projects, “Walking with Minerals” and “Critical Clay,” Lilja have invited participants to encounter mineral matter in more nuanced ways rather than just as passive resources to be exploited. Drawing from the feminist posthumanities, geology, and agential realism, Lilja introduces “diffractive practices of design”—a relational approach examining mineral matter and materials’ complex entanglements that extend beyond human-centered perspectives. Petra Lilja has been a doctoral student at Konstfack and KTH 2019-2024. Coming from an industrial design education, her practice sits in the borderlands of art, craft and technology, with a focus on exploring design’s role in reconfiguring ethical and material relationships in shared processes of more-than-human world-making. Petra Lilja, at Art, Technology and Design, Konstfack, University of Arts, Crafts and Design, Stockholm, and KTH, Royal Institute of Technology, Stockholm. Friday November 29th, 13:30 CET Konstfack, Mandelgren: LM Ericssons väg 14, Stockholm Online: https://konstfack-se.zoom.us/j/63502413639', '# Mineral Matterings: Diffractive Practices of Design PETRA LILJA: PUBLIC DEFENSE OF DOCTORAL DISSERTATION This dissertation, Mineral Matterings: Diffractive Practices of Design makes design perceptible as a source of concern, part of the extractivist logic. Through two projects, “Walking with Minerals” and “Critical Clay,” Lilja have invited participants to encounter mineral matter in more nuanced ways rather than just as passive resources to be exploited. Drawing from the feminist posthumanities, geology, and agential realism, Lilja introduces “diffractive practices of design”—a relational approach examining mineral matter and materials’ complex entanglements that extend beyond human-centered perspectives. Petra Lilja has been a doctoral student at Konstfack and KTH 2019-2024. Coming from an industrial design education, her practice sits in the borderlands of art, craft and technology,');
