, and Research through Art and Design. Kristine Diekman is a media artist and educator. As an educator, she facilitates international workshops in digital storytelling and physical computing that lead participants through writing, craft, computing, and sound production to create interactive tactile audio interfaces to tell their stories. Dr. Svenja Keune is the VIBRA Research Network manager and facilitator of the I.N.S.E.C.T. Community and summer camp co-creations. She is a researcher always weaving new connections in between materials, practices, people, and fields of research.', 'Sources/Raw/designposthumanism/post-2878-tactile-stories-project.md', 'designposthumanism', 'https://designandposthumanism.org/archives/2878');
INSERT INTO source_chunks(id, title, url, source_family, path, body, description) VALUES (65, 'Book launch: More-than-Human Design in Practice', 'https://designandposthumanism.org/archives/2905', 'designposthumanism', 'Sources/Raw/designposthumanism/post-2905-book-launch-more-than-human-design-in-practice-2.md', '# Book launch: More-than-Human Design in Practice Save the date! An online book launch event will take place on 28th January from 16:00-17:00 CET. Place: https://aalto.zoom.us/j/64406010435 There will be a panel with chapter authors and celebratory rituals with a more-than-human twist. The panel will present concrete and practical takeaways based on the chapters they have written. We then discuss: what are the next steps for more-than-human design? How can it scale? Panel guests are: Michelle Westerlaken, Erik Sandelin, Heidi Biggs, Iohanna Nicenboim and Martín Tironi. “This book provides an overview of the diverse multidisciplinary field of more-than-human design, offering a philosophical grounding of more-than-human design in posthumanism while putting practical design examples and methods to the forefront. There is an urgent need to radically re-imagine design, as its current processes are contributing to global warming, pollution, deforestation, ocean acidification, ozone layer depletion, loss of biodiversity and species extinction. Given this need, ‘more-than-human design’ has emerged as a perspective that widens our thinking beyond solely human-oriented considerations and needs, such as animals, plants and microbes. The book explores the relationship between sustainability and design, touching on topics such as AI, systems thinking, futures studies and pedagogy, and discusses a range of case study projects that are grounded in more-than-human thinking, demonstrating how this can be incorporated into practice. This easily accessible and theoretically grounded book will provide design researchers and educators an excellent introduction to more-than-human thinking. It will also be of interest to students and scholars studying design more broadly, sustainability, environmental studies and service design, as well as to practicing designers interested in sustainability.” Preorder the book now! Several D+PH members are initiators and contributors to this book.', '# Book launch: More-than-Human Design in Practice Save the date! An online book launch event will take place on 28th January from 16:00-17:00 CET. Place: https://aalto.zoom.us/j/64406010435 There will be a panel with chapter authors and celebratory rituals with a more-than-human twist. The panel will present concrete and practical takeaways based on the chapters they have written. We then discuss: what are the next steps for more-than-human design? How can it scale? Panel guests are: Michelle Westerlaken, Erik Sandelin, Heidi Biggs, Iohanna Nicenboim and Martín Tironi. “This book provides an overview of the diverse multidisciplinary field of more-than-human design, offering a philosophical grounding of more-than-human design in posthumanism while putting practical design examples and methods to the forefront. There is an urgent need to radically re-imagine design, as its current processe');
INSERT INTO source_chunks_fts(title, body, path, source_family, url) VALUES ('Book launch: More-than-Human Design in Practice', '# Book launch: More-than-Human Design in Practice Save the date! An online book launch event will take place on 28th January from 16:00-17:00 CET. Place: https://aalto.zoom.us/j/64406010435 There will be a panel with chapter authors and celebratory rituals with a more-than-human twist. The panel will present concrete and practical takeaways based on the chapters they have written. We then discuss: what are the next steps for more-than-human design? How can it scale? Panel guests are: Michelle Westerlaken, Erik Sandelin, Heidi Biggs, Iohanna Nicenboim and Martín Tironi. “This book provides an overview of the diverse multidisciplinary field of more-than-human design, offering a philosophical grounding of more-than-human design in posthumanism while putting practical design examples and methods to the forefront. There is an urgent need to radically re-imagine design, as its current processes are contributing to global warming, pollution, deforestation, ocean acidification, ozone layer depletion, loss of biodiversity and species extinction. Given this need, ‘more-than-human design’ has emerged as a perspective that widens our thinking beyond solely human-oriented considerations and needs, such as animals, plants and microbes. The book explores the relationship between sustainability and design, touching on topics such as AI, systems thinking, futures studies and pedagogy, and discusses a range of case study projects that are grounded in more-than-human thinking, demonstrating how this can be incorporated into practice. This easily accessible and theoretically grounded book will provide design researchers and educators an excellent introduction to more-than-human thinking. It will also be of interest to students and scholars studying design more broadly, sustainability, environmental studies and service design, as well as to practicing designers interested in sustainability.” Preorder the book now! Several D+PH members are initiators and contributors to this book.', 'Sources/Raw/designposthumanism/post-2905-book-launch-more-than-human-design-in-practice-2.md', 'designposthumanism', 'https://designandposthumanism.org/archives/2905');
INSERT INTO source_chunks(id, title, url, source_family, path, body, description) VALUES (66, 'HUMAN-DE-CENTRED DESIGN. Towards a (new) era of suffering', 'https://designandposthumanism.org/archives/2910', 'designposthumanism', 'Sources/Raw/designposthumanism/post-2910-human-de-centred-design-towards-a-new-era-of-suffering.md', '# HUMAN-DE-CENTRED DESIGN. Towards a (new) era of suffering To tackle the global challenges of the Anthropocene, this paper proposes the concept of Human-de-centred Design, a new design approach aimed at decisively moving beyond Human-centred Design. Positioned as a stronger alternative to similar attempts from the posthumanism field, this new paradigm promotes a vision in which the needs of ‘non-hu-man agents’ are prioritised. This leads to a proactive removal of the user from the centre of the design process, requiring that human interests be subordinated to the ecosystem’s needs. To achieve this post-anthropocentric revolution, the concept of the ‘era of suffering’ is introduced, intended to highlight the efforts required from humanity. To illustrate this concept, three case studies are analysed, demonstrating how design can challenge traditional logic and adopt a more radical perspective that removes the human user from the centre of the project. By Niccolò Casiddu, Francesco Burlando, and Boyu Chen francesco.burlando@unige.it The paper is published on AGATHÓN – International Journal of Architecture, Art and Design | n. 16 | 2024 | pp. 242-249', '# HUMAN-DE-CENTRED DESIGN. Towards a (new) era of suffering To tackle the global challenges of the Anthropocene, this paper proposes the concept of Human-de-centred Design, a new design approach aimed at decisively moving beyond Human-centred Design. Positioned as a stronger alternative to similar attempts from the posthumanism field, this new paradigm promotes a vision in which the needs of ‘non-hu-man agents’ are prioritised. This leads to a proactive removal of the user from the centre of the design process, requiring that human interests be subordinated to the ecosystem’s needs. To achieve this post-anthropocentric revolution, the concept of the ‘era of suffering’ is introduced, intended to highlight the efforts required from humanity. To illustrate this concept, three case studies are analysed, demonstrating how design can challenge traditional logic and adopt a more radical perspec');
INSERT INTO source_chunks_fts(title, body, path, source_family, url) VALUES ('HUMAN-DE-CENTRED DESIGN. Towards a (new) era of suffering', '# HUMAN-DE-CENTRED DESIGN. Towards a (new) era of suffering To tackle the global challenges of the Anthropocene, this paper proposes the concept of Human-de-centred Design, a new design approach aimed at decisively moving beyond Human-centred Design. Positioned as a stronger alternative to similar attempts from the posthumanism field, this new paradigm promotes a vision in which the needs of ‘non-hu-man agents’ are prioritised. This leads to a proactive removal of the user from the centre of the design process, requiring that human interests be subordinated to the ecosystem’s needs. To achieve this post-anthropocentric revolution, the concept of the ‘era of suffering’ is introduced, intended to highlight the efforts required from humanity. To illustrate this concept, three case studies are analysed, demonstrating how design can challenge traditional logic and adopt a more radical perspective that removes the human user from the centre of the project. By Niccolò Casiddu, Francesco Burlando, and Boyu Chen francesco.burlando@unige.it The paper is published on AGATHÓN – International Journal of Architecture, Art and Design | n. 16 | 2024 | pp. 242-249', 'Sources/Raw/designposthumanism/post-2910-human-de-centred-design-towards-a-new-era-of-suffering.md', 'designposthumanism', 'https://designandposthumanism.org/archives/2910');
INSERT INTO source_chunks(id, title, url, source_family, path, body, description) VALUES (67, 'PLANETARY EXPERIMENTS: ENVIRONMENTS BY DESIGN', 'https://designandposthumanism.org/archives/2917', 'designposthumanism', 'Sources/Raw/designposthumanism/post-2917-planetary-experiments-environments-by-design.md', '# PLANETARY EXPERIMENTS: ENVIRONMENTS BY DESIGN SYMPOSIUM: June 13, 2025 Technische Universität Dresden hybrid event Annual Conference of the German Society for Design Theory and Research (deu: Deutsche Gesellschaft für Designtheorie und -forschung, DGTF) The German Society for Design Theory and Research (DGTF), in collaboration with the Chair of Digital Cultures TU Dresden, invites participants to critically engage with the theme Planetary Experiments: Environments by Design. This conference explores the diverse perspectives and practices of design research and theory in addressing the far-reaching ecological, social and technological challenges of our time. Over the course of one day and in a hybrid setup (Dresden & remote), we will bring together scholars and practitioners to engage in keynote talks, panel discussions, and practice presentations that critically explore the concept of Planetary Experiments. Planetary Experiments serves as a multidisciplinary lens for examining humanity’s profound interventions in the Earth’s ecosystems and climate. It positions design as both a contributor to the planet’s crises and a pivotal force for driving its transformation. These experiments encompass a broad spectrum of activities, from deliberate ecological manipulations such as deep-sea mining, land reclamation or rewilding, to speculative technological interventions in geoengineering, synthetic biology and data-driven climate adaptation. As the scope of our technological capacities advances, we stand at a critical junction where interventions once confined to the laboratory are being scaled to the planetary level, transforming the way we understand, design and inhabit environments. Planetary Experiments seeks to map the complexities of planetary-scale design interventions and interrogate the ethical, social and ecological stakes of reimagining the planet as a site of experimentation and design. At the heart of the conference is an inquiry into the expanding methodological landscapes of design research. Contributions from international experts in design, architecture, media and science and technology studies will highlight the transformative potential of interdisciplinary, practice-based approaches to generate new forms of knowledge that transcend traditional academic silos. In an era marked by overlapping crises, the conference fosters dialogue around emerging practices that bridge disciplines and actively engage with the Earth and its future habitability. DGTF in cooperation with the Chair of Digital Cultures as TU Dresden Conference Team Michaela Büsse (TU Dresden) Johanna Mehl (TU Dresden) Bianca Herlo (DGTF) Lynn Harles (DGTF) If you have any questions, please reach out to symposium2025@dgtf.de More information www.planetary-experiments.dgtf.de Key visual DESIGN EARTH (Rania Ghosn and El Hadi Jazairy), The Planet After Geoengineering, 18/25, 2021.', '# PLANETARY EXPERIMENTS: ENVIRONMENTS BY DESIGN SYMPOSIUM: June 13, 2025 Technische Universität Dresden hybrid event Annual Conference of the German Society for Design Theory and Research (deu: Deutsche Gesellschaft für Designtheorie und -forschung, DGTF) The German Society for Design Theory and Research (DGTF), in collaboration with the Chair of Digital Cultures TU Dresden, invites participants to critically engage with the theme Planetary Experiments: Environments by Design. This conference explores the diverse perspectives and practices of design research and theory in addressing the far-reaching ecological, social and technological challenges of our time. Over the course of one day and in a hybrid setup (Dresden & remote), we will bring together scholars and practitioners to engage in keynote talks, panel discussions, and practice presentations that critically explore the concept of ');
