e foundations for our human ways to connect to each other and the environment, expanded by a specific focus on a local ecosystem and related companion species (a method we developed inspired by Haraways Camille Stories). The two perspectives are worked with through the focus groups Mapping Ecologies, Becoming, and Critical Making. Commitment and registration/application Our co-creation ranges from setting the food menu to deciding together how we structure our days. Therefore, we are looking for participants who can contribute enthusiastically to planning before (from May onwards online), generating during (August 24 -30 in person), and evaluating after (from September online) the I.N.S.E.C.T. Summercamp 2025. In order to curate a vibrant group, we would like to get to know you better and therefore ask you to answer this form. We have in between 15 and 30 spaces to offer, and want to gather a lively mix of people. We also want to make sure that we have enough capacity for the preparation and planning prior to the camp and also for aftercare, reflection, processing and documentation. To register/apply please fill out this form. I.N.S.E.C.T. Community members are favoured if they register before April 15th. Please indicate in the form if you are an alumni or newcomer. We will be accepting applications until April 30th and will contact you in the first week of May with next steps. Accommodation and Fees We have bunk beds for 35 people in the scoutcamp. Mattresses are provided, pillows and sleeping bags/ duvets, blankets and towels need tobe brought by attendees. You are also welcome to bring your tent or van. The fee for the duration of the camp is on a sliding scale between 300 and 600€, based on income, and includes 3 vegetarian meals/day plus snacks. Fees must be paid in full by June 21st. Travel to and from the venue and insurance is the responsibility of the attendees. The registration / application is now open! We will process your information as soon as possible and will respond to you via email by the end of April. lf you have additional questions, please reach out to us via insectworldings@gmail.com or svenja.keune@hb.se.', 'Sources/Raw/designposthumanism/post-2939-i-n-s-e-c-t-summercamp-resonating-worlds.md', 'designposthumanism', 'https://designandposthumanism.org/archives/2939');
INSERT INTO source_chunks(id, title, url, source_family, path, body, description) VALUES (70, 'Co-Creation MtH learning session: More-than-Human Design in Practice', 'https://designandposthumanism.org/archives/2950', 'designposthumanism', 'Sources/Raw/designposthumanism/post-2950-co-creation-mth-learning-session-more-than-human-design-in-practice.md', '# Co-Creation MtH learning session: More-than-Human Design in Practice Kincentric Leadership in association with The Repatterning Collective is organising an learning series as part of a Co-creation with the More-than-Human sandbox. The ‘sandbox’ provides a virtual place for experiential learning, where we can shine a light on different approaches & explore their development and application, their workings, and future directions, to advance our understanding of and ability to genuinely co-create with the more-than-human. The next session on April 9th will explore the practical applications of more-than-human design. More-than-human design goes beyond balancing human needs with those of other living beings—it also considers the trade-offs between the well-being of entire ecosystems and the needs of specific species or individual organisms. What benefits one may not always benefit the whole. Donna Haraway refers to this challenge as “staying with the trouble,” but when applied to concrete design projects, these dilemmas become tangible, requiring designers to make choices that inevitably prioritize some needs over others. Designing and making always involve trade-offs. This session will discuss methods and concepts that designers are developing to navigate these complexities. Our speaker Anton Poikolainen Rosén is a postdoctoral researcher in Human-Computer Interaction at the Department of Computer and System Sciences, Stockholm University. He studies design for sustainable futures and the more-than-human world. His research themes include farming and waste management from critical, circular and multisensory perspectives. Anton is co-editor of the book More-than-Human Design in Practice, which he worked with during his postdoc at the Department of Design, Aalto University, Helsinki. Want to be informed of upcoming learning sessions? Register for the sandbox’s mailing list. Next season More about the series', '# Co-Creation MtH learning session: More-than-Human Design in Practice Kincentric Leadership in association with The Repatterning Collective is organising an learning series as part of a Co-creation with the More-than-Human sandbox. The ‘sandbox’ provides a virtual place for experiential learning, where we can shine a light on different approaches & explore their development and application, their workings, and future directions, to advance our understanding of and ability to genuinely co-create with the more-than-human. The next session on April 9th will explore the practical applications of more-than-human design. More-than-human design goes beyond balancing human needs with those of other living beings—it also considers the trade-offs between the well-being of entire ecosystems and the needs of specific species or individual organisms. What benefits one may not always benefit the whol');
INSERT INTO source_chunks_fts(title, body, path, source_family, url) VALUES ('Co-Creation MtH learning session: More-than-Human Design in Practice', '# Co-Creation MtH learning session: More-than-Human Design in Practice Kincentric Leadership in association with The Repatterning Collective is organising an learning series as part of a Co-creation with the More-than-Human sandbox. The ‘sandbox’ provides a virtual place for experiential learning, where we can shine a light on different approaches & explore their development and application, their workings, and future directions, to advance our understanding of and ability to genuinely co-create with the more-than-human. The next session on April 9th will explore the practical applications of more-than-human design. More-than-human design goes beyond balancing human needs with those of other living beings—it also considers the trade-offs between the well-being of entire ecosystems and the needs of specific species or individual organisms. What benefits one may not always benefit the whole. Donna Haraway refers to this challenge as “staying with the trouble,” but when applied to concrete design projects, these dilemmas become tangible, requiring designers to make choices that inevitably prioritize some needs over others. Designing and making always involve trade-offs. This session will discuss methods and concepts that designers are developing to navigate these complexities. Our speaker Anton Poikolainen Rosén is a postdoctoral researcher in Human-Computer Interaction at the Department of Computer and System Sciences, Stockholm University. He studies design for sustainable futures and the more-than-human world. His research themes include farming and waste management from critical, circular and multisensory perspectives. Anton is co-editor of the book More-than-Human Design in Practice, which he worked with during his postdoc at the Department of Design, Aalto University, Helsinki. Want to be informed of upcoming learning sessions? Register for the sandbox’s mailing list. Next season More about the series', 'Sources/Raw/designposthumanism/post-2950-co-creation-mth-learning-session-more-than-human-design-in-practice.md', 'designposthumanism', 'https://designandposthumanism.org/archives/2950');
INSERT INTO source_chunks(id, title, url, source_family, path, body, description) VALUES (71, 'Ocean comm/uni/ty Festival 2025', 'https://designandposthumanism.org/archives/2981', 'designposthumanism', 'Sources/Raw/designposthumanism/post-2981-ocean-comm-uni-ty-festival-2025.md', '# Ocean comm/uni/ty Festival 2025 Call for participation: Ocean comm/uni/ty Festival 2025, at TBA21–Academy Ocean comm/uni/ty Festival 2025 June 16–27, 2025 | Online and open to the public. Hosted by TBA21–Academy | Community Curator: Lígia Oliveira, D&PH Network member This June, following the UN Ocean Conference, TBA21–Academy will host the Ocean comm/uni/ty Festival 2025: a digital gathering where art, ecology, design, and imagination intertwine in service of the ocean and regenerative futures. Curated as a living digital ecosystem, the festival explores three interwoven themes: Imagination (seeds): Envisioning possible futures for and with the ocean through speculative and regenerative thinking. Kinship (blooms): Deepening our connection to the ocean through collective practices like storytelling, listening, contemplation, and shared experience. Action (harvests): Generating tangible contributions — be it a manifesto, poem, ritual, artwork or a collection — to the Ocean-Archive.org and beyond. Contributions may include talks, workshops, listening sessions, poetic gestures, digital rituals or experimental formats. Honoraria of €150 are available for selected contributors. Call for participation deadline: Sunday, 18 May 2025 Full details: Ocean comm/uni/ty Open Call Submission form: Submit your proposal', '# Ocean comm/uni/ty Festival 2025 Call for participation: Ocean comm/uni/ty Festival 2025, at TBA21–Academy Ocean comm/uni/ty Festival 2025 June 16–27, 2025 | Online and open to the public. Hosted by TBA21–Academy | Community Curator: Lígia Oliveira, D&PH Network member This June, following the UN Ocean Conference, TBA21–Academy will host the Ocean comm/uni/ty Festival 2025: a digital gathering where art, ecology, design, and imagination intertwine in service of the ocean and regenerative futures. Curated as a living digital ecosystem, the festival explores three interwoven themes: Imagination (seeds): Envisioning possible futures for and with the ocean through speculative and regenerative thinking. Kinship (blooms): Deepening our connection to the ocean through collective practices like storytelling, listening, contemplation, and shared experience. Action (harvests): Generating tangible');
INSERT INTO source_chunks_fts(title, body, path, source_family, url) VALUES ('Ocean comm/uni/ty Festival 2025', '# Ocean comm/uni/ty Festival 2025 Call for participation: Ocean comm/uni/ty Festival 2025, at TBA21–Academy Ocean comm/uni/ty Festival 2025 June 16–27, 2025 | Online and open to the public. Hosted by TBA21–Academy | Community Curator: Lígia Oliveira, D&PH Network member This June, following the UN Ocean Conference, TBA21–Academy will host the Ocean comm/uni/ty Festival 2025: a digital gathering where art, ecology, design, and imagination intertwine in service of the ocean and regenerative futures. Curated as a living digital ecosystem, the festival explores three interwoven themes: Imagination (seeds): Envisioning possible futures for and with the ocean through speculative and regenerative thinking. Kinship (blooms): Deepening our connection to the ocean through collective practices like storytelling, listening, contemplation, and shared experience. Action (harvests): Generating tangible contributions — be it a manifesto, poem, ritual, artwork or a collection — to the Ocean-Archive.org and beyond. Contributions may include talks, workshops, listening sessions, poetic gestures, digital rituals or experimental formats. Honoraria of €150 are available for selected contributors. Call for participation deadline: Sunday, 18 May 2025 Full details: Ocean comm/uni/ty Open Call Submission form: Submit your proposal', 'Sources/Raw/designposthumanism/post-2981-ocean-comm-uni-ty-festival-2025.md', 'designposthumanism', 'https://designandposthumanism.org/archives/2981');
INSERT INTO source_chunks(id, title, url, source_family, path, body, description) VALUES (72, 'The Nordic Summit of Species, Aarhus 2025', 'https://designandposthumanism.org/archives/2991', 'designposthumanism', 'Sources/Raw/designposthumanism/post-2991-the-nordic-summit-of-species-aarhus-2025.md', '# The Nordic Summit of Species, Aarhus 2025 By the howl of the wolf, the wingbeats of the swan, by the whisper of the birch and the trickling of the melting waters we call all friends and defenders of the living world to gather for the Nordic Summit of Species! We share the planet with a multitude of more-than-human neighbours: Birds, rivers, insects, forests, fungi and beyond. In this summit we come together to listen to our neighbours, to learn from them and advocate for their rights. The Nordic Summit of Species is a two-day event bringing together civil society organisations that are advocating for rights and representation of nature. Each with their own approach to lending their voices to more-than-human agents. You will meet ambassadors for bumblebees and swans as well as artists, academics and activists advocating for glaciers and rivers. We invite a diverse array of voices and we would love your voice to be there too! www.summitofspecies.org Summit program and registration If you’re interested in joining the summit and participating in the events, tickets are available now. The last day to register your interest is August 22. For questions, please contact D+PH members Inna Zrajaeva or John Kazior from Feral Malmö or Thomas Laurien. Aarhus, Denmark, 12-13 September 2025 Organizers: Arternes Ambassadører, Arternas ambassad Viskan-Borås, Snæfellsjökul fyrir forseta, OsloMet, Feral Malmö, Organismen Demokratie, and VIA University College. Funded by Demos/Nordic Culture Point', '# The Nordic Summit of Species, Aarhus 2025 By the howl of the wolf, the wingbeats of the swan, by the whisper of the birch and the trickling of the melting waters we call all friends and defenders of the living world to gather for the Nordic Summit of Species! We share the planet with a multitude of more-than-human neighbours: Birds, rivers, insects, forests, fungi and beyond. In this summit we come together to listen to our neighbours, to learn from them and advocate for their rights. The Nordic Summit of Species is a two-day event bringing together civil society organisations that are advocating for rights and representation of nature. Each with their own approach to lending their voices to more-than-human agents. You will meet ambassadors for bumblebees and swans as well as artists, academics and activists advocating for glaciers and rivers. We invite a diverse array of voices and we');
