n person at Transit, Telefonplan, register: https://forms.gle/RmxrgTRf9vrkxPvf8We meet at the entrance of Konstfack, LM Ericssons väg 14, Stockholm 17:45 on June 2nd and we will walk you to the location, be on time! – Or join us online – https://teams.microsoft.com/dl/launcher/launcher.html?… Thanks to Gundega Strauberga (visualisation), Johannes Rydinger (film) andAlbin Karlsson (Programming)!', 'Sources/Raw/designposthumanism/post-2997-skogsparlamentet-council-of-forest.md', 'designposthumanism', 'https://designandposthumanism.org/archives/2997');
INSERT INTO source_chunks(id, title, url, source_family, path, body, description) VALUES (74, 'Postdoc call: Design after Progress', 'https://designandposthumanism.org/archives/3018', 'designposthumanism', 'Sources/Raw/designposthumanism/post-3018-postdoc-call-design-after-progress.md', '# Postdoc call: Design after Progress A postdoc position in the research environment Design after Progress: Reimagining Design Histories and Futures (DaP). Deadline: Monday 16th June. Find the announcement here – in English or switch to Swedish: https://lnu.se/en/meet-linnaeus-university/work-with-us/vacancies-page/ Subject area for the position: Design, with a focus on socio-ecological and post-anthropocentric emerging design practices, within the framework of the research environment Design after Progress: Reimagining Design Histories and Futures. Location until further notice: Department of Design, Faculty of Arts and Humanities, Växjö, Sweden. Since Linnaeus University is located in both Växjö and Kalmar, travel between the two may be required, as well as presence in Malmö, southern Sweden, where a part of the project collaborators are located. Hours and terms: 100 % employment for two years. The position may be extended by one year in accordance with the postdoctoral agreement. Starting date: Starting date as agreed upon or as soon as possible. Job description: The tasks mainly consist of research in design, with a focus on socio-ecological and post-anthropocentric design methods. You will actively participate in both external and internal networks of the department. The aim of the position is to contribute to both individual and collaborative design research within the Design after Progress research environment, which inquires into alternative design histories and futures beyond progress-oriented perspectives, to explore socio-ecological justice. The research environment consists of three design studios and aims to develop knowledge and methods for designing with care for degraded habitats as well as contribute to design education. Your work will be centred in Design Studio Two, where you will explore and implement design projects aiming to develop and test prefigurative methods grounded in participatory design. An important part of the position is to plan and implement experimental study circles with designers worldwide, in hybrid format to develop prototypes of how design can respond to socio-ecological challenges. The study circles may take place through a combination of digital and in-person meetings. You will also facilitate seminars and critically reflect on your own and the collaborative research projects through publication. Teaching and supervision, comprising no more than 20% of the total workload, shall be included among your duties. Best wishes from the DaP-team: Åsa Ståhl, Kristina Lindström, Li Jönsson, Thomas Laurien and Maria Göransdotter', '# Postdoc call: Design after Progress A postdoc position in the research environment Design after Progress: Reimagining Design Histories and Futures (DaP). Deadline: Monday 16th June. Find the announcement here – in English or switch to Swedish: https://lnu.se/en/meet-linnaeus-university/work-with-us/vacancies-page/ Subject area for the position: Design, with a focus on socio-ecological and post-anthropocentric emerging design practices, within the framework of the research environment Design after Progress: Reimagining Design Histories and Futures. Location until further notice: Department of Design, Faculty of Arts and Humanities, Växjö, Sweden. Since Linnaeus University is located in both Växjö and Kalmar, travel between the two may be required, as well as presence in Malmö, southern Sweden, where a part of the project collaborators are located. Hours and terms: 100 % employment for t');
INSERT INTO source_chunks_fts(title, body, path, source_family, url) VALUES ('Postdoc call: Design after Progress', '# Postdoc call: Design after Progress A postdoc position in the research environment Design after Progress: Reimagining Design Histories and Futures (DaP). Deadline: Monday 16th June. Find the announcement here – in English or switch to Swedish: https://lnu.se/en/meet-linnaeus-university/work-with-us/vacancies-page/ Subject area for the position: Design, with a focus on socio-ecological and post-anthropocentric emerging design practices, within the framework of the research environment Design after Progress: Reimagining Design Histories and Futures. Location until further notice: Department of Design, Faculty of Arts and Humanities, Växjö, Sweden. Since Linnaeus University is located in both Växjö and Kalmar, travel between the two may be required, as well as presence in Malmö, southern Sweden, where a part of the project collaborators are located. Hours and terms: 100 % employment for two years. The position may be extended by one year in accordance with the postdoctoral agreement. Starting date: Starting date as agreed upon or as soon as possible. Job description: The tasks mainly consist of research in design, with a focus on socio-ecological and post-anthropocentric design methods. You will actively participate in both external and internal networks of the department. The aim of the position is to contribute to both individual and collaborative design research within the Design after Progress research environment, which inquires into alternative design histories and futures beyond progress-oriented perspectives, to explore socio-ecological justice. The research environment consists of three design studios and aims to develop knowledge and methods for designing with care for degraded habitats as well as contribute to design education. Your work will be centred in Design Studio Two, where you will explore and implement design projects aiming to develop and test prefigurative methods grounded in participatory design. An important part of the position is to plan and implement experimental study circles with designers worldwide, in hybrid format to develop prototypes of how design can respond to socio-ecological challenges. The study circles may take place through a combination of digital and in-person meetings. You will also facilitate seminars and critically reflect on your own and the collaborative research projects through publication. Teaching and supervision, comprising no more than 20% of the total workload, shall be included among your duties. Best wishes from the DaP-team: Åsa Ståhl, Kristina Lindström, Li Jönsson, Thomas Laurien and Maria Göransdotter', 'Sources/Raw/designposthumanism/post-3018-postdoc-call-design-after-progress.md', 'designposthumanism', 'https://designandposthumanism.org/archives/3018');
INSERT INTO source_chunks(id, title, url, source_family, path, body, description) VALUES (75, 'Confluence of European Water Bodies #3', 'https://designandposthumanism.org/archives/3024', 'designposthumanism', 'Sources/Raw/designposthumanism/post-3024-confluence-of-european-water-bodies-3.md', '# Confluence of European Water Bodies #3 The third edition of the Confluence of European Water Bodies will take place in the Netherlands, from 21 – 24 September 2025. Famous author Robert Macfarlane will open the Confluence, Sunday afternoon September 21 2025 in De Balie Amsterdam, with a talk about his new book Is A River Alive? Whether it’s the Amazon or Amstel, our language, laws, and economies still treat rivers as resources — channels to be dredged, diverted, and controlled. In Is A River Alive?, Macfarlane explores the deep cultural, ecological, and spiritual bonds between people and waterways. Recognizing rivers as living entities, he argues, is essential to restoring our damaged ecosystems. It calls for a radical reimagining of law, language, and land — a shift from dominion to kinship. Organised by the Embassy of the North Sea, in collaboration with De Balie and the water bodies of Europe, we explore how the Rights of Nature can reshape Europe’s legal, cultural, and political landscapes. Drop, Ripple, Puddle, O On September 21st, we will open with a choral ritual titled Drop, Ripple, Puddle, O (shortened as O). O is a participatory composition that explores the sensorial materials of languages—visual, sonic, gestural. As the Confluence joins 35 grassroots movements advocating for the representation of regional waterbodies, O will thread the many represented human languages into our invocation. The composition will follow the lead of our host waterbody, brackish water from Singelgracht, which will be present within a cymatic sound sculpture. The collective Snæfellsjökul fyrir forseta (Glacier for president) has devised the opening ritual for the 2025 Confluence. Snæfellsjökul fyrir forseta is Iceland’s first Rights of nature movement, having nominated Snæfellsjökull for the 2024 Icelandic presidency. Collective members co-devising O are Daniela Amado, Stijn Brinkman, Corinna Duschl, Linnéa Falck, Galadriel González Romero, Maja Jantar, Vena Naskręcka, angela Snæfellsjökuls rawlings, Daria Testo, and Magdalena Tworek. Water Bodies 2025 are: Akerselva, Baltic Sea, Deep Sea, Dogger Bank, Drina, Genova waters, Irish Sea & Liffey, Klarälven, Laguna de la Janda, Loire, Maas, Mar Menor, Mediterranean, North Sea, Odra, Os_lo Fjord, Ouse, Pek, Piave, Reuss, Reva, Rhine, Rhône, Snæfellsjökul, Spree, Tara, Tagliamento, Tavignanu, Tejo, Ticino, Vättern, Venice lagoon, Viskan, Vistula, Wattenmeer and Zenne Tickets & Info Sunday 21 September 2025 / 15.00 – 17.00 De Balie, independent platform for contemporary art, politics, and science. Kleine Gartmanplantsoen 10, 1017 RR Amsterdam. Editor and moderator De Balie: Kees Foekema. In collaboration with the Embassy of the North Sea and Uitgeverij Athenaeum. Ticket sale will open soon The Confluence of European Water Bodies was founded in 2023 by the Embassy of the North Sea, ILP Mar Menor and TBA21–Academy. The edition 2025 is supported by Cultuurfonds, VriendenLoterij Fonds, Forest Peace and Chalmers University Sweden. D+PH member Thomas Laurien is representing Lake Vättern and River Viskan in the Confluence of European Water Bodies 2025.', '# Confluence of European Water Bodies #3 The third edition of the Confluence of European Water Bodies will take place in the Netherlands, from 21 – 24 September 2025. Famous author Robert Macfarlane will open the Confluence, Sunday afternoon September 21 2025 in De Balie Amsterdam, with a talk about his new book Is A River Alive? Whether it’s the Amazon or Amstel, our language, laws, and economies still treat rivers as resources — channels to be dredged, diverted, and controlled. In Is A River Alive?, Macfarlane explores the deep cultural, ecological, and spiritual bonds between people and waterways. Recognizing rivers as living entities, he argues, is essential to restoring our damaged ecosystems. It calls for a radical reimagining of law, language, and land — a shift from dominion to kinship. Organised by the Embassy of the North Sea, in collaboration with De Balie and the water bodies');
