care—and how reimagining heritage can open space for more just and diverse pasts, presents, and futures. Dr Thomas Laurien is an artist/designer/curator, educator and researcher based at HDK-Valand – Academy of Art and Design at the University of Gothenburg. He is also the chair of the advocacy group The Natureculture Association Shimmer and Entanglements, and co-founder of one of its initiatives, the Species Embassy Viskan-Borås. His research and artistic practice focus on issues of more-than-human representation, and multispecies justice/Rights of Nature. Laurien is an active member of the Confluence of European Water Bodies network where he tries to make the Swedish water bodies Lake Vättern and the watershed Viskan present, and where a collective learning process is taking place on how to implement the Rights of Nature in European contexts. He is also part of the organising team that arranged the Nordic Summit of Species in Aarhus in September 2025. This session’s moderator: Satu Samira Hamed Video call link: https://meet.google.com/cvg-ctxc-toz', 'Sources/Raw/designposthumanism/post-3353-dph-tools-methods-session-2.md', 'designposthumanism', 'https://designandposthumanism.org/archives/3353');
INSERT INTO source_chunks(id, title, url, source_family, path, body, description) VALUES (93, 'Food and Art Alternative MA: Open Call 2026-2028', 'https://designandposthumanism.org/archives/3371', 'designposthumanism', 'Sources/Raw/designposthumanism/post-3371-food-and-art-alternative-ma-open-call-2026-2028.md', '# Food and Art Alternative MA: Open Call 2026-2028 The Food and Art Alternative MA (altMA) is a two-year programme that explores food practices as political and cultural subjects within contemporary art. Combining weekly online seminars with five ten-day international residencies, the course supports interdisciplinary research, collective inquiry, and sustained project development. Positioning food at the intersection of culinary histories, socially engaged art, political discourse, and ecological action, the altMA examines how food operates as a site of power, memory, labour, and speculation. The curriculum moves across food politics, curatorial studies, historical analysis, and contemporary artistic practice, encouraging critical reflection and experimental methodologies. Teaching is primarily delivered online through lectures, guest presentations, peer-led discussions, group critiques, and individual tutorials. Each term, the cohort convenes for a ten-day residency focused on practice-based research, workshops, and fieldwork. Confirmed locations include Amsterdam (with Mediamatic), Mexico City (with colectivo amasijo), and Madrid. These residencies are central to deepening collective bonds and advancing individual and collaborative projects. Throughout the programme, participants receive targeted support through one-to-one tutorials and peer cross-crits. In the second year, the cohort develops an optional collective public presentation of their research, which may take the form of a dinner, exhibition, publication, screening, or other experimental format. The altMA cultivates a committed learning community grounded in trust, critical engagement, and shared inquiry, bringing together practitioners and thinkers invested in food as a medium for artistic, social, and ecological transformation. Important dates: 01 Feb – Food and Art altMA applications open 15 Mar – Food and Art altMA applications close 31 May – altMA places offered and enrolment 01 Jul – Pre-entry materials sent 18 Sep – Course begins with a residency in Mediamatic (Amsterdam) For further information, please visit: www.thegramounce.com Instagram: @thegramounce Email: kitchen@thegramounce.com September 2026 – May 2028 Cohort: 35 participants Faculty: Nora Silva, Inês Neto dos Santos, Noa Jansma, Kevin Bellò (D+PH member), Inês Coelho da Silva, and Barney Pau. Guest speakers: Asunción Molinos Gordo, Aaron Cezar (Delphina Foundation), Institute for Genomic Gastronomy, Eduardo Castillo-Vinuesa (Foodscapes), Michael Marder, Salma Serry, Grandeza Studio, Rowan Deer, dr. masharu, Patricia Kaishian, Federico Campagna, Abena Offeh-Gyimah, Suzanne Bernhardt & Philipp Kolmann, colectivo amasijo, and more.', '# Food and Art Alternative MA: Open Call 2026-2028 The Food and Art Alternative MA (altMA) is a two-year programme that explores food practices as political and cultural subjects within contemporary art. Combining weekly online seminars with five ten-day international residencies, the course supports interdisciplinary research, collective inquiry, and sustained project development. Positioning food at the intersection of culinary histories, socially engaged art, political discourse, and ecological action, the altMA examines how food operates as a site of power, memory, labour, and speculation. The curriculum moves across food politics, curatorial studies, historical analysis, and contemporary artistic practice, encouraging critical reflection and experimental methodologies. Teaching is primarily delivered online through lectures, guest presentations, peer-led discussions, group critiques');
